sible upper bound for the third-order Hankel determinant, namely 0.104485, which is not sharp. ... At this value of ℓ, the function {d}_{9}(\\mathsf{\\ell }) reaches its highest value, which is\nThe third-order determinant is expressed as\n\n{\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)=2{b}_{2}{b}_{3}{b}_{4}-{b}_{3}^{3}-{b}_{4}^{2}+{b}_{3}{b}_{5}-{b}_{2}^{2}{b}_{5}.\n\n(2)\n\nZaprawa [4] investigated the third Hankel determinant and obtained non-sharp bounds, namely |{\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)|\\le 1 for starlike functions and |{\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)|\\le 49/540 for convex functions. Since the formula of {\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right) involves higher-order coefficients, deriving exact estimates is technically demanding, and achieving sharpness becomes essential. Despite these difficulties, Kowalczyk et al. [5] succeeded in 2018 in establishing the sharp bound |{\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)|\\le 4/135 for the convex class. For starlike functions, the same upper estimate |{\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)|\\le 4/135 was later reported by Kwon et al. [6]. In the same year, Lecko et al. [7] demonstrated that the sharp bound |{\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)|\\le 1/9 holds for starlike functions of order 1/2. The following Table 1 provides a survey of results concerning the computation of the third-order Hankel determinant for various classes of functions. It indicates whether the proposed bound is sharp, and if not, whether a conjecture for the sharp bound was formulated and subsequently proved by another researcher. We highlight the cases in which the bound was not sharp but a conjecture for the sharp bound was proposed, yet remained unproven. In this paper, we establish the proof of one such conjecture, while leaving our methodology available to researchers for addressing the other conjecture, which likewise remains an open problem.\n\nLupaş et al. [17] introduced the following class of functions, which is also presented in Table 1:\n\n{S}